---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const path = '/use-cases/deliverability';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/deliverability');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/deliverability');
const comparisonApps = [
  'sequenzy', 'klaviyo', 'sendlane', 'brevo', 'omnisend', 'drip',
  'mailchimp', 'activecampaign', 'shopify-email', 'yotpo-email-sms',
].map((s) => appBySlug(s)!).filter(Boolean);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<article class="container prose-shopify py-16">
<a href="/use-cases">All use cases</a>
<h1>Diagnose email and SMS delivery separately</h1>
<p>Delivery failures need channel-specific evidence. An email accepted by a receiving server is not proof of inbox placement, and an SMS delivery receipt is not proof that a customer read it. This is a diagnostic workflow, not a platform ranking or a claim that switching apps repairs reputation.</p>
<h2>Scope the incident</h2><p>Record the affected channel, sender identity, recipient provider or destination, message type, first failure time, and recent changes. Compare marketing with order notifications and separate rejection, deferral, bounce, complaint, and unsubscribe signals. Preserve representative error codes and message identifiers for support; remove customer details from broadly shared incident notes.</p>
<h2>Check email authentication on an actual message</h2><p>Inspect received headers and the supplier's domain setup instructions. Confirm the intended From domain and authentication alignment rather than assuming a published DNS record proves every route is correct. Recheck after a DNS change or platform migration. Do not add a second SPF record as a shortcut.</p>
<p><a href="https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126?hl=en">Google's sender requirements</a> distinguish all senders from bulk senders to personal Gmail accounts. The bulk requirements include SPF, DKIM, DMARC, alignment, and one-click unsubscribe for marketing and subscribed messages. Apply the requirements relevant to your traffic; authentication alone does not promise inbox placement.</p>
<h2>Investigate audience sources</h2><p>Break failures down by acquisition source, signup period, and recent activity. Quarantine an unexplained import while investigating permission records and suppression history. A customer profile or prior order alone is not evidence of SMS marketing permission. Do not use a re-engagement blast to ask an unverified list whether it wanted the first blast.</p>
<h2>Contain and resume deliberately</h2><p>Pause the affected promotional path while preserving necessary service notifications through a reviewed route. Coordinate with the supplier when deferrals or reputation problems appear. Define a monitored restart audience and expansion criteria using actual response trends; there is no universal percentage ramp or ten-day cure. Keep an operator able to stop queued messages.</p>
<h2>Use a separate SMS investigation</h2><p>Inspect the provider's reason codes, sender registration status where applicable, destination eligibility, message segmentation, and opt-out handling. Compare carriers and countries before blaming creative. Confirm that a withdrawn subscriber stays suppressed across imports and retries. Request the supplier's current requirements for the affected route instead of borrowing email warm-up advice.</p>
<h2>Choose support by the evidence it exposes</h2><p>Ask prospective suppliers to demonstrate raw delivery events, suppression exports, authentication setup, incident escalation, and what data survives migration. Clarify shared versus dedicated infrastructure and ownership of domains or numbers. A unified dashboard can help operations but does not itself protect transactional reputation from promotional mistakes.</p>
<h2>Close with a reproducible record</h2><p>Document the suspected cause, change made, observation window, and remaining uncertainty. Monitor by provider and source after resuming. Seed messages can reveal a symptom but are not a population-wide placement measurement. Avoid judging inactivity only from opens; privacy features and automated activity can distort them.</p>
<p><a href="/use-cases/black-friday">Black Friday operations</a> manages peak campaign collisions. <a href="/use-cases/browse-abandonment">Browse follow-up</a> validates event relevance. Neither replaces this channel-level incident investigation.</p>
<h2>App discovery and demonstration checklist</h2>
<p>Use these profiles as starting points. Ask each supplier to demonstrate this workflow with your events, markets, permissions, and failure cases before buying. Listing is not a verified feature comparison or performance ranking; confirm current plans and destination charges directly.</p>
<ul>{comparisonApps.map(app => <li><a href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></li>)}</ul>
</article>
</BaseLayout>
